api/b17-closed-period-write-path.test.js
  closing a pay period: who may, what is refused, and what is logged
  ok       W1. an OWNER closes a pay period: 200, ONE appended row carrying the period and its paid_at, no upsert, and the derived set comes back
  ok       W2. the BILLING (office-manager/payroll) role closes a pay period: 200. Michael named "owner plus office manager / payroll person" and billing IS this app's back-office/payroll tier
  ok       W3. a scheduler is REFUSED 403 and writes NO row
  ok       W3. a coordinator is REFUSED 403 and writes NO row
  ok       W3. a caregiver is REFUSED 403 and writes NO row
  ok       W5. ABLATION -- with MANAGEMENT_ROLES widened to include scheduler, a scheduler is STILL refused the close. SEN_PERIOD_CLOSE_ROLES is the gate, not the general back-office bucket, and this is the only arm under which the difference is observable
  ok       W6. re-writing an existing closing_id (here: moving its paid_at later, which would make an owed adjustment vanish) is 409 CLOSING_IMMUTABLE, writes nothing, and the message names the reopen route
  ok       W7. CONTROL -- appending a SECOND closing alongside the first is 200, so W6 is the immutability rule and not a branch that refuses everything
  ok       W7b. closing the SAME period under a new closing_id is 409 PERIOD_ALREADY_CLOSED -- one period, one freeze instant
  ok       W8. a closing whose paid_at cannot be read is 400 INVALID_CLOSING at the WRITE, not a pay run that refuses forever afterwards
  ok       W9. a missing closing_id is 400 and is NOT generated server-side -- the id is what makes a retried request a refusal instead of a duplicate
  ok       W9b. kind "delete" is 400 in the handler -- the senpc_kind CHECK in the schema is the second defence, not the first
audit log write failed: pay_period_closed 400 violates check constraint "sairnsenior_audit_log_event_type_check"
  ok       W10. a FAILED audit write REFUSES the close (503 AUDIT_UNAVAILABLE) and writes NO closing. api/_lib/audit.js is best-effort for credential grants on purpose -- a logging outage must not lock somebody out of their own app -- and the trade is the OTHER WAY for a wage freeze, which is retryable a minute later. The message names the file to run
  ok       W11. the audit row names the event, the employee, the role AND the period and instant that were frozen -- "settings changed" would not let anybody reconstruct which wage stopped being restatable
  ok       W12. a FAILED read of the existing ledger is 503 and writes nothing -- treating it as empty would let a closing be replaced silently, which is the defect this ledger exists to prevent
  ok       W12b. a FAILED read of the LEGACY settings row also refuses, and the message names which of the two sources could not be read
  ok       W12c. with the table absent the READ is an honest empty and the INSERT answers 503 NOT_PROVISIONED naming the schema file -- today's expected live answer, since neither SQL file has been run
  ok       W13. BOTH event types this branch emits are INSIDE the audit table's CHECK constraint. An event_type missing from a CHECK is a silently dropped audit row everywhere else on this platform; here it is a refusal, so it would be loud -- but it would still block every close
  ok       W14. SEN_PERIOD_CLOSE_ROLES is { owner, billing } and is a SEPARATE object from MANAGEMENT_ROLES, declared with its own roleSet() call. Aliasing it would reintroduce exactly the silent widening W5 drives
  ok       W15. sen_period_closings is REGISTERED in api/_resources/sairnsenior.js, so the provisioning probe can see it and the dispatcher can reach it

  20 passed, 0 failed, 0 unproven
